# /data/config/w2c.env — the PLATFORM's half of this box's environment: where its stores are, who this server is,
# its secrets. Every unit reads it, and `vms.env` (the VMS subsystems' settings) after it. On the data partition,
# never in a rootfs slot (М9 Lesson 5): an OS update must not change which store this box is a member of.
# ================================================================================================
# NOTES — what every part of this file does and why (kept beside the code, not in a separate document)
# ================================================================================================
# # w2c.env.example — `/data/config/w2c.env`: the platform's settings, the half every unit reads first
#
# **Role.** The platform is w2c, the VMS is one of its subsystems (the product's rule, 3 October): what a process
# needs to be a member of the platform — its store, its server's name, its labels, its key — is here, in one file
# that does not change when a subsystem is added; what a VMS process needs to do its work — the archive's root,
# capacity, the media directory, the backfill window — is in `vms.env`. Every Quadlet unit names both,
# `EnvironmentFile=/data/config/w2c.env` then `EnvironmentFile=/data/config/vms.env` (a name set in both: the second
# wins). The product's pair is `/etc/w2c/w2c.env` and `/etc/vms/vms.env`; the course keeps its box's root,
# `/data/config/`, for the reason above. `tests/test_deploy_units.py` checks that every unit reads the two and that
# each variable is in its own half.
#
# - `PLATFORM_DIR` — the platform's two stores under one directory: `<dir>/config` (FileVariables — rows,
#   assignments, placement, epochs, slots, idempotency keys) and `<dir>/objects` (FsObjectStore — heartbeats, the
#   snapshot). Every unit mounts `/data/platform` at the same path. In М11 the rows move to `configstore`.
# - `PLATFORM_STORE` — the config store, said as a URL and nothing else (`w2cplatform.variables.store_url`; its old
#   name `CONFIG_URL` is still read). Unset: the files under `$PLATFORM_DIR/config`, in-process — on one box there
#   is no daemon, no hop and no second quorum. In a cluster: `configstore:///run/configstore/<role>.sock`.
# - What a RUNTIME hands every process by name, so that none of them learns which runtime it was
#   (`w2cplatform/runtime.py`): `SERVER_NAME` (whose resource this process writes into; unset: the hostname),
#   `LABELS` (what this server can reach, comma-separated; unset: none), `BOX_ID` (this machine's id, the host part
#   of a recorder's hold). Quadlet sets `WORKER_NAME=%i` per unit and nothing else — on one box the hostname is the
#   server and there are no labels, so the three are commented out below.
# - Secrets: the cluster's key ring is a FILE, `/data/secrets/platform.key` (`python3 -m w2cplatform.sealing new`),
#   never a value here — this file is read by every unit, the key goes only into the three that open sealed fields
#   (the console, the holder, the recorder), mounted at `/run/secrets/platform.key` with `SECRETS_KEY` set in each
#   of those units.
# - The spares' script (`w2c-spares.sh`, a platform script run by `w2c-spares*.timer`) reads this file too:
#   `CONSOLE` and the ceilings per role.
# ================================================================================================
PLATFORM_DIR=/data/platform
# PLATFORM_STORE=file:///data/platform/config
# SERVER_NAME=
# LABELS=
# BOX_ID=
# the spares' script: where the console answers, and how many spares of each role this server may run
# CONSOLE=http://127.0.0.1:8080
# MAX_RECORDERS=8
# MAX_WORKERS=4
# MAX_GATEWAYS=4
# MAX_EVALUATORS=4
